.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/globals Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "globals"
.PP
.RS 4
Global identifiers from different JavaScript environments 
.RE
.PP

.PP
It's just a \fRJSON file\fP, so you can use it in any environment\&.

.PP
This package is used by ESLint 8 and earlier\&. For ESLint 9 and later, you should depend on this package directly in \fRyour ESLint config\fP\&.
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install globals
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
import globals from 'globals';

console\&.log(globals\&.browser);
/*
{
    addEventListener: false,
    applicationCache: false,
    ArrayBuffer: false,
    atob: false,
    …
}
*/
.fi
.PP

.PP
Each global is given a value of \fRtrue\fP or \fRfalse\fP\&. A value of \fRtrue\fP indicates that the variable may be overwritten\&. A value of \fRfalse\fP indicates that the variable should be considered read-only\&. This information is used by static analysis tools to flag incorrect behavior\&. We assume all variables should be \fRfalse\fP unless we hear otherwise\&.

.PP
For Node\&.js this package provides two sets of globals:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRglobals\&.nodeBuiltin\fP: Globals available to all code running in Node\&.js\&. These will usually be available as properties on the \fRglobalThis\fP object and include \fRprocess\fP, \fRBuffer\fP, but not CommonJS arguments like \fRrequire\fP\&. See: https://nodejs.org/api/globals.html
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRglobals\&.node\fP: A combination of the globals from \fRnodeBuiltin\fP plus all CommonJS arguments ("CommonJS module scope")\&. See: https://nodejs.org/api/modules.html#modules_the_module_scope
.PP

.PP
When analyzing code that is known to run outside of a CommonJS wrapper, for example, JavaScript modules, \fRnodeBuiltin\fP can find accidental CommonJS references\&. 
